Specify microscopic states macroscopic questions and statistical premises (@physics-statistical-states: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Derive-subsystem-fluctuations-under-a-fixed-total-energy.lingua#r_0070FE92B084AF3217F03BAC9F") { r_00CF73DE0F1F6E72DAF547A67B
A macroscopic description retains selected quantities: energy, pressure, composition or a measured response. A microscopic model specifies enough detail to calculate those quantities under its laws. The relationship is many-to-one. A measured total energy generally does not identify all constituent states, and a probability distribution is additional information about their preparation or statistical description. Reuse [Derive a wall pressure from momentum transfer and velocity moments](Derive-a-wall-pressure-from-momentum-transfer-and-velocity-moments.lingua#r_0E97E7965A096183F866D6F07C): its finite velocity table determines certain pressure moments, but matching those moments did not establish equilibrium or a full distribution.

Begin with N labeled, localized sites, each having two stipulated states x_i=0 or 1, with energies 0 and ε>0. A configuration is the ordered binary list x=(x_1,…,x_N); its energy is E(x)=εΣx_i. Neglect interaction energy in this account. These are model assumptions, not a derivation of atomic spectra or quantum measurement. Labeled localized sites permit this counting; a gas of indistinguishable particles requires different state accounting. The excitation is a state label, not a conserved material particle.

Write q=Σx_i. The macrostate q collects Ω_N(q)=C(N,q) configurations, by choosing the q excited sites. This follows from [Count by disjoint cases products and explicit bijections](../../mathematics/core/Count-by-disjoint-cases-products-and-explicit-bijections.lingua#r_00310CC3F2A2C1C4D9E6CF5397). For N=4, the multiplicities for q=0,…,4 are 1,4,6,4,1. Their sum is 16, because every binary list is counted once. The phrase most numerous refers to this declared grouping, not an intrinsic visual disorder.

An isolated preparation with exactly q excitations restricts the accessible set. A microcanonical model assigns equal probability to each admitted configuration, so p(x)=1/C(N,q). Equal weights are a statistical premise whose applicability depends on accessible states, conserved quantities, dynamics and preparation. Equal energy alone does not prove that a physical system explores the entire set, loses its history or samples it uniformly. Additional conserved quantities, barriers or insufficient observation time can change the appropriate description.

Distinguish four averages. A particle average is computed across constituents of one configuration. An ensemble expectation Σp(x)A(x) averages an observable over the specified distribution. A sample average uses actual observations. A time average follows a trajectory. Their agreement needs conditions; none becomes the other by renaming it. [Describe a chance quantity by its weighted mean and spread](../../inquiry/core/Describe-a-chance-quantity-by-its-weighted-mean-and-spread.lingua#r_0A9487B256CD8C0ED0083290DA) provides the probability algebra without making physical sampling assumptions. In this block the finite tables are stipulated ensembles and the later dynamics are explicitly defined models, not measured matter.

**Work:** list all six configurations with N=4,q=2. Separate their common energy from the energy of the first two sites. Compare a uniform distribution over the six configurations with a preparation concentrated on 1100. Both conserve the same total energy; identify an observable that distinguishes them. Read [Statistical Physics — David Tong](../../engineering/materials-engineering/sources/Statistical-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_7FMG47HD1MT706NT3CABRTFNS2) progressively, chapter 1 beginning with sections 1.1–1.2; retain the preparation limits stated here.

**Continue:** [Derive subsystem fluctuations under a fixed total energy](Derive-subsystem-fluctuations-under-a-fixed-total-energy.lingua#r_0070FE92B084AF3217F03BAC9F).

